Subject: [PATCH] nvme-pci: log PCI state around FLR recovery (diagnostic only)

Diagnostic patch on top of 0070, no behaviour change. Drop once the
question below is answered.

0070's logging showed the controller reading back a command register of
~0 right after pcie_flr() returned, and the recovery only works because
config space is restored again later from nvme_pci_enable(). What it
cannot tell us is which of two very different things the controller is
doing at that moment, because a config read of the command register
returns ~0 in both cases:

  - Answering Request Retry Status, i.e. "still initialising, come back".
    That is legal, and the root port synthesizes ~0 to complete the read
    for every register except the Vendor ID.

  - Not completing config requests at all. Per PCIe r4.0 sec 6.6.2 a
    function may only discard requests while its FLR is in progress, so
    this far past the FLR it is a violation.

The Vendor ID separates them. The root port on this system reports
RootCtl CRSVisible+, so Configuration RRS Software Visibility is enabled
and a Vendor ID read that got RRS is synthesized as 0x0001 rather than
~0. A Vendor ID of ~0 therefore means the controller is not answering.

Sample the Vendor ID, command register, and the root port's link and
device status at three points: right after pcie_reset_flr() returns and
before pci_restore_state() does any config access of its own, on entry
to the recovery path in nvme_pci_enable(), and if that path gives up.
Also report how many restore attempts the settle loop needed.

Reading the log:

  vid low half 0x0001	controller is in RRS, behaving legally but slower
			than pcie_flr() allows for; a host-side problem
  vid ~0		controller is not completing config requests; a
			controller firmware problem
  rp lnksta bit 13	Data Link Layer Link Active, clear means the link
			dropped rather than requests being discarded
  rp lnksta bit 11	Link Training
  rp devsta bit 3	Unsupported Request Detected

The interesting line is "flr returned", since that is the state
pci_dev_wait() judged to be ready. If it shows a Vendor ID of ~0 then
pci_dev_wait() mistook a silent controller for a ready one, because its
RRS branch only tests for the 0x0001 marker and treats everything else,
including ~0, as ready. The enumeration path guards against exactly that
in pci_bus_generic_read_dev_vendor_id() before testing for RRS.

---
diff -Nur a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
@@ -2669,6 +2669,34 @@
 	return true;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Diagnostic for post-FLR recovery. The Vendor ID is the interesting field:
+ * with Configuration RRS Software Visibility enabled on the root port, a low
+ * half of 0x0001 means the controller answered Request Retry Status, i.e. it
+ * is still initialising but is completing config requests as the spec
+ * requires. ~0 instead means it is not completing them at all, which it may
+ * only do while an FLR is still in progress. The root port's link and device
+ * status distinguish a dropped link from requests being discarded.
+ */
+static void nvme_pci_log_reset_state(struct nvme_dev *dev, const char *when)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
+	struct pci_dev *bridge = pci_upstream_bridge(pdev);
+	u16 cmd, lnksta = 0, devsta = 0;
+	u32 vid;
+
+	pci_read_config_dword(pdev, PCI_VENDOR_ID, &vid);
+	pci_read_config_word(pdev, PCI_COMMAND, &cmd);
+	if (bridge) {
+		pcie_capability_read_word(bridge, PCI_EXP_LNKSTA, &lnksta);
+		pcie_capability_read_word(bridge, PCI_EXP_DEVSTA, &devsta);
+	}
+
+	dev_info(dev->ctrl.device,
+		 "flr diag %s: vid %#010x cmd %#06x rp lnksta %#06x devsta %#06x\n",
+		 when, vid, cmd, lnksta, devsta);
+}
+
 static int nvme_pci_enable(struct nvme_dev *dev)
 {
 	int result = -ENOMEM;
@@ -2683,9 +2711,12 @@
 	if (readl(dev->bar + NVME_REG_CSTS) == -1) {
 		unsigned long settle;
 		bool responded = false;
+		unsigned int attempts = 0;
 		u16 vendor, cmd;
 		u32 bar0;
 
+		nvme_pci_log_reset_state(dev, "csts unreadable");
+
 		/*
 		 * A controller can keep re-applying its reset defaults for a
 		 * while after pcie_flr()'s pci_dev_wait() saw config space
@@ -2702,6 +2733,8 @@
 
 		settle = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(NVME_FLR_SETTLE_MS);
 		for (;;) {
+			attempts++;
+
 			/*
 			 * pci_restore_state() only clears state_saved, the
 			 * saved copy itself stays valid, so re-arm the flag to
@@ -2725,15 +2758,16 @@
 
 		if (!responded) {
 			dev_warn(dev->ctrl.device,
-				 "registers unreachable after flr: vid %#06x cmd %#06x bar0 %#010x\n",
-				 vendor, cmd, bar0);
+				 "registers unreachable after flr: vid %#06x cmd %#06x bar0 %#010x (%u attempts)\n",
+				 vendor, cmd, bar0, attempts);
+			nvme_pci_log_reset_state(dev, "gave up");
 			result = -ENODEV;
 			goto disable;
 		}
 
 		dev_info(dev->ctrl.device,
-			 "registers responded after restoring config space: cmd %#06x bar0 %#010x\n",
-			 cmd, bar0);
+			 "registers responded after restoring config space: cmd %#06x bar0 %#010x (%u attempts)\n",
+			 cmd, bar0, attempts);
 	}
 
 	/*
@@ -2866,6 +2900,13 @@
 				u16 cmd;
 
 				/*
+				 * Sample the state pci_dev_wait() decided was
+				 * ready, before the restore does any config
+				 * access of its own.
+				 */
+				nvme_pci_log_reset_state(dev, "flr returned");
+
+				/*
 				 * Re-arm the saved copy around the restore so
 				 * the reset path can restore it again if the
 				 * device drops its config space once more.
-- 
2.34.1
